;; MMIO mapping admission: may this physical range be mapped at all?
;;
;; `aiueos_map_pci_mmio` in kernel/paging.c opened with a pure predicate over
;; its two arguments, evaluated before a single page-table byte was touched.
;; Everything after that predicate -- resolving the PDPT, claiming a directory
;; slot, writing 2 MiB PTEs, `invlpg` -- is mechanism and stays in C. The
;; predicate is not: it is the decision about which physical addresses this
;; kernel is willing to make addressable, and it was the last one still living
;; inside that mechanism. This object owns it now.
;;
;; The C wrapper calls this first and returns 0 unchanged when it answers 0, so
;; a rejected range performs NO page-table work -- as before, and for the same
;; reason: the failure mode of admitting a bad range is not a wrong return
;; value, it is a PTE that makes some other physical page writable through a
;; device window.
;;
;; THE CLAUSES (each is a clause the C had; none is new, none is dropped.
;; They are written as the conditions for ADMITTING, where the C wrote the
;; conditions for refusing, and the two length clauses are adjacent here where
;; the C had them apart. The disjunction is over total, side-effect-free
;; expressions, so neither the order nor the polarity changes the answer.)
;;
;;   length > 0            A zero-length range has no pages. The C spelled this
;;                         `!length`; see SIGNEDNESS below for why it is
;;                         written `<= 0` here.
;;   length <= 1 GiB       One mapping may not exceed a directory's reach.
;;   address >= 1 GiB      Below 0x40000000 is DRAM, the kernel image, the
;;                         framebuffer and the low-memory trampolines -- all
;;                         already mapped by their owners. A device window is
;;                         never down there, so a request that says it is, is
;;                         wrong about something, and mapping it uncached and
;;                         writable would alias memory somebody else owns.
;;   address < 2^40        The paging code below assumes a 40-bit physical
;;                         aperture: it takes a PML4 index from bits 39+ and a
;;                         PDPT index from bits 38:30.
;;   address + length      Wraparound. Unreachable here; kept deliberately.
;;     >= address          See SIGNEDNESS.
;;   address + length      The ceiling applies to the END of the range, not
;;     <= 2^40             only to its start -- otherwise a range beginning
;;                         just under 2^40 would run past the aperture.
;;   one 1 GiB region      The last clause, and the one that is easy to read as
;;                         redundant next to `length <= 1 GiB`. It is not.
;;
;; WHY THE 1 GiB CONFINEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING
;;
;; The caller walks the page tables ONCE:
;;
;;   pdpt_index = (address >> 30) & 0x1ff;   /* computed from the START */
;;   ... claim one directory for that one slot ...
;;   for (page = first; ; page += 2 MiB) directory[(page >> 21) & 0x1ff] = ...
;;
;; The PDPT index is taken from the first address and never recomputed, while
;; the loop runs to the LAST page. A range that begins at 0x7ff00000 and is
;; 2 MiB long is only 2 MiB -- it passes `length <= 1 GiB` -- but it straddles
;; the 1 GiB boundary at 0x80000000, and the loop's second iteration would index
;; the directory of the region it started in, wrapping `(page >> 21) & 0x1ff`
;; back to 0 and writing a PTE for the WRONG gigabyte. The caller would report
;; success and the device window would be half-mapped over whatever else lives
;; in the first 2 MiB of the starting region.
;;
;; So `length <= 1 GiB` bounds the work and this clause bounds the WALK. Losing
;; it would let a caller ask for a mapping the mechanism cannot follow, and the
;; mechanism has no way to notice. It is reproduced here in full.
;;
;; SIGNEDNESS -- the C is unsigned, this object is signed i64
;;
;; Every clause above is a comparison against a bound below 2^41, so the two
;; arithmetics agree on the whole admissible region and disagree only about how
;; each REJECTS the inadmissible one:
;;
;;   * A `uint64_t` above 2^63 arrives here as a negative i64. In C such an
;;     address failed `address >= 2^40`; here it fails `address >= 1 GiB` from
;;     the other side, because it is negative. Rejected either way.
;;   * A negative `length` is, to the C, a huge unsigned length and fails
;;     `length > 1 GiB`. Signed, it would pass that test, so the zero test is
;;     written `<= 0` rather than `= 0`: it absorbs the C's `!length` and the
;;     negative case in one clause. This is the one place where a literal
;;     transcription of the C would have been WRONG.
;;   * The wraparound clause `address + length < address` detects, in the C,
;;     the sum crossing 2^64. In signed i64 the sum would have to cross 2^63,
;;     and it cannot: the clauses before it cap `address` below 2^40 and
;;     `length` at 2^30, so the sum is under 2^41 -- twenty-two bits of
;;     headroom. It is retained anyway, and not because it can fire. It is
;;     retained because it is a bound-consistency check that a future edit to
;;     those caps would need to keep true, and because the object should be
;;     total over its own i64 signature rather than over the two clauses that
;;     happen to precede it today. Dropping it would cost nothing now and
;;     silently remove a guard later; keeping it costs one add and one compare
;;     in an object called a few dozen times per boot.
;;
;; There is no shift in this subset, so `>> 30` is `quot` by 2^30. The two
;; agree here because both operands are non-negative wherever the result is
;; consulted: `quot` truncates toward zero and `>>` floors, and they differ only
;; on negatives, which the `address >= 1 GiB` clause has already rejected.

(defn aiueos-mmio-map-admit [address length]
  (if (or (<= length 0)
          (> length 1073741824)
          (< address 1073741824)
          (>= address 1099511627776)
          (< (+ address length) address)
          (> (+ address length) 1099511627776)
          (not (= (quot address 1073741824)
                  (quot (- (+ address length) 1) 1073741824))))
    0
    1))

(defn main [] 0)
